## Step-by-Step Feature Branch Execution

The following step-by-step terminal routine outlines the complete life cycle of an isolated feature branch within a team.

### Step 1: Sync and Update local Environment
Before starting any development, sync your local repository with the remote server to guarantee you are building on top of the latest, most stable code.
```bash
git checkout main
git pull origin main
```

### Step 2: Establish a Distinct Feature Branch
Create and switch to a targeted branch. Use clear, descriptive names prefixed by the nature of the task (e.g., `feature/`, `bugfix/`, or `art/`).
```bash
git checkout -b feature/player-movement
```

### Step 3: Stage and Commit Incremental Work
Make regular, granular commits as you hit milestones. Write your messages in the imperative, active voice.
```bash
# Verify modified and untracked files (check for matching .meta files!)
git status

# Stage your modified C# script along with its meta data
git add Assets/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs Assets/Scripts/PlayerMovement.cs.meta

# Commit with a concise summary of the functional change
git commit -m "Implement basic keyboard WASD input controls for player movement"
```

### Step 4: Publish to the Remote and Open a Pull Request
Push your local tracking branch up to GitHub to prepare for the review process.
```bash
git push -u origin feature/player-movement
```
*Note: The `-u` (upstream) flag links your local feature branch directly to the remote branch for all subsequent pushes and pulls.*

### Step 5: Merge and Safely Clean Up
Once your Pull Request passes continuous testing and is formally approved by a team member on GitHub, merge the branch into `main` using the GitHub web interface. Afterwards, return to your terminal to clean up your workspace:
```bash
# Shift back to the main branch
git checkout main

# Pull down the newly merged code from the remote server
git pull origin main

# Safely delete the local feature branch that is no longer needed
git branch -d feature/player-movement
```

